e they're reacting more to  the current crisis on their plate and  how to deal with that nina is it a fact  that the government may be waiting till  later in the year to see really how bad  the situation gets before deciding on  any long-term action  that is possible but the risk is that if  they wait too long the situation will  get even worse than it would have gotten  if they act sooner so i think there is  already enough evidence to suggest and  to show just how many households are  struggling the economists are unanimous  in saying that inflation will stay  elevated for for longer um you have also  economic forecasters including um  including the bank of england expecting  a recession so i think there is already  sufficient evidence to suggest that it's  time to act now rather than put people  through further hardship before before  something is done all right talking of  hardship darren says i have a choice  council tax or electricity and gas and  food it's not hard to choose the winner  um assuming people are across the board  having to make choices aren't they  that's right and i think um really what  this  example shows from darren is that it's  there's no there's no longer really  choices anymore these are just  impossible decisions about which  essentials they can cover it's not about  having a buffer where you can start  tightening your purse strings and and  decide what to you know where to  prioritize your your finances  what people are telling us when they  come to food banks is that there aren't  any further cutbacks to make they're  sort of out of options and also food  bank managers are telling us that they  also feel that they're out of options  too that people are doing all they can  you know that they're trying to ensure  that they're claiming everything that  they're eligible for  they're budgeting  um they're prioritizing you know  what  they're on their household spending um  but there's nothing there's nothing  really left to  do to manage the cost of living crisis  at the moment  and actually it we do we have seen that  in our network for a number of years now  for some people but what's really  concerning is that this is obviously  accelerating and gatherings step in and  show that they recognize their sort of  emergency that's really unfolding now  for many people in our communities all  right we'll come back to you very  shortly adam says on the 5th of november  2020 the government cancelled the job  retention bonus a 1 000 pound incentive  to employers to retain staff and  continue topping up their furlough what  happened to the retention incentive that  failed to materialize the many thousands  of pounds employers are owed have a  massively detrimental effect on  businesses is there any intention adam  asks to repay  the debts uh nina let's put that one to  you  i certainly don't think there is any  intention of bringing that back and i  think the the chancellor has been quite  definitive in the way he's talked about  that the justification being that that  was cancelled because the furlough  scheme was extended now you could it's  certainly valid to ask questions about  is it uh is it reasonable is it fair to  make an announcement have businesses  make business decisions on the back of  that announcement and then basically say  that  that the things aren't that things have  changed and that that assistance won't  be forthcoming i would say  uh  in aggregate that the furlough scheme in  the uk was very generous and lasted for  quite a long time so hopefully most  businesses would have benefited that  needed at that time would have benefited  from the ability to keep staff on  furlough for longer and were were able  to get some form of assistance if not  that specific thousand pounds per per  employee coming back from furlough okay  five a question we are a couple this is  through twitter earning a total of 35  000 pounds annually after tax we are  bringing up three children spending on  basics and still saving some for  holidays why can't other people start  acting  wisely a quick one for you laura that's  pretty unfair to many people that i  would many might suggest yes i think so  i think but it probably echoes the views  of some people out there who think well  i'm doing fine prices have gone up a  little bit but i can still afford it  everyone else should just budget and i  think it just matters depends massively  on your circumstances um how you came  into this cost of living crisis whether  the pandemic impacted your finances  dramatically or not and so i think while  that person is doing very well um other  people are really struggling and it's  not just a case of cutting back on  netflix or shopping at a cheaper shop  and final word to you timmy in a quick  sentence when you hear that why can't  other people start acting wisely what do  you think  i think for some people they are  fortunate enough to have a buffer to um  choose you know to change their spending  decisions for people on the very lowest  incomes people that need to use food  banks um they don't have that option  they don't have the luxury of that  option  and that's why we need government action  to ensure that people are protected from  the very worst of the crisis that's  unfolding at the moment all right uh  sumi rabindra kumar nina scaro and laura  suter thank you very much indeed for  joining us on our special q a program  today  you're watching sky news tonight coming  up in the next hour russia's ambassador  to the eu tell sky news the expansion of  nato to include finland would be a  threat to the stability of europe  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

